==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|scherzo||joke, play|lang=it}}, from {{term|scherzare||to joke, jest|lang=it}} from Old {{etyl|it|-}} {{term|scherzare|lang=it}}, from {{etyl|lng|en}} {{recons|skerzan||to jump merrily, enjoy oneself, jest|lang=lng}} from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|skirtaną||to hop, jump|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|(s)kerǝd-||to dance, jump|lang=ine-pro}}. Akin to {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term|scherzen|lang=gmh}} "to frolic, jump merrily, hop up and down" ({{etyl|de|-}} {{term|scherzen|lang=de}} "to joke"; {{term|Scherz|lang=de}} "joke, sport"), {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|skjerta|lang=no}} "to joke".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈskɛːtsəʊ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl=scherzos|pl2=scherzi}}

# {{context|music|lang=en}} A piece of music or a [[movement]] from a larger piece such as a [[symphony]]; especially, a piece of music played in a playful manner.
#* '''1980''', Anthony Burgess, ''Earthly Powers'':
#*: At seven Val knocked – three shorts and one long, out of the '''scherzo''' of Beethoven’s Fifth – and I rushed to open.

